# EmComm radio interface binding — copy to configs/radio.conf and edit.
#
# Per-unit hardware, so configs/radio.conf is gitignored: a by-id path carries
# the interface's serial number, which is machine data and must not land in the
# repository. This .sample ships; your real file does not — configs/*.conf is
# already gitignored here, as everything in configs/ is.
#
# The provisioner never opens either device. Opening a serial port asserts its
# control lines, and on an interface whose PTT is wired to one of them that
# puts a radio on the air. You name them; the provisioner substitutes and
# checks that the paths exist, nothing more.
#
#
# ADEVICE — the sound card Direwolf uses for receive and transmit audio.
#
#   List what the machine has. Read-only, opens nothing:
#       arecord -l
#       cat /proc/asound/cards
#
#   Card NUMBERS move when another USB audio device is attached, so prefer the
#   card-id form. Both are valid Direwolf syntax:
#
#       ADEVICE=plughw:CARD=Device,DEV=0        # by card id — preferred
#       ADEVICE=plughw:1,0                      # by index — moves
#
#   The id is the name in square brackets in /proc/asound/cards. A generic
#   CM108-class interface usually reports "Device"; a second one becomes
#   "Device_1", so check rather than assume when two are attached.
#
#
# PTT_DEVICE / PTT_METHOD — how Direwolf keys the radio.
#
#   Digirig Mobile is a USB hub carrying TWO devices: a CM108-based sound card
#   and a CP2102 serial bridge. Its PTT is an open-collector switch driven by
#   the RTS line of that serial port — NOT a CM108 GPIO. `PTT CM108` therefore
#   does not key this hardware, however plausible it looks given the audio chip.
#
#       ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/          # with the interface attached
#
#       PTT_DEVICE=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Silicon_Labs_CP2102_USB_to_UART_Bridge_Controller_EXAMPLE-if00-port0
#       PTT_METHOD=RTS
#
#   Use the by-id path, not /dev/ttyUSB0. A GPS puck and a radio interface both
#   enumerate as ttyUSB*, and which one gets ttyUSB0 depends on the order they
#   were plugged in — so a numbered path can hand gpsd your radio interface, or
#   hand Direwolf your GPS.
#
#   PTT_METHOD accepts what Direwolf accepts:
#       RTS  DTR        assert the line to key
#       -RTS -DTR       inverted, for interfaces wired the other way
#       CM108           GPIO on a CM108-class sound chip; PTT_DEVICE is then a
#                       /dev/hidraw* path, or empty to let Direwolf find one
#
#
# UNTESTED. Nothing in this repository has keyed a radio. Watch the radio key
# before you rely on the station to transmit, and read the note about docking
# and power-on in the operator's manual first — an interface that keys on RTS
# can transmit the moment something opens its port.

ADEVICE=
PTT_DEVICE=
PTT_METHOD=RTS
